1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Falling, gravity's victim.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,000
Falling becomes floating.

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:14,040
For fighter pilots, smoke jumpers, thrill seekers,

4
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:18,060
and even interplanetary robots.

5
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:21,960
Now, parachutes on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The American Pronunciation Guide Presents

7
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,000
to Pronounce Parachute Business

8
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,140
People in the parachute business like to say

9
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,920
it takes a rocket to get something into space,

10
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:49,960
but it takes a parachute to get it safely back down.

11
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:57,460
On July 4, 1997, NASA's pilot pilot

12
00:00:57,461 --> 00:01:01,960
On July 4, 1997, NASA's Pathfinder parachuted safely

13
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:06,700
onto Mars and beamed images back to Earth.

14
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:10,780
36 years earlier, Alan Shepard made it safely back to Earth

15
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,180
after his 15-minute suborbital flight in Freedom 7.

16
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:21,380
In advertising, drama and suspense

17
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,860
reach new heights when parachutes are used.

18
00:01:23,861 --> 00:01:28,860
On June 6, 1944, upwards of 20,000 paratroopers,

19
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:32,960
the largest focused drop of parachutes in history,

20
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:35,600
paved the way for the success of D-Day.

21
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:40,140
I think most people probably believe that everything

22
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:44,240
to know about parachutes was learned back about World War II,

23
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:45,780
but that certainly isn't true.

24
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:49,280
They've made tremendous strides since then,

25
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:51,220
new materials, new techniques.

26
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:57,160
Parachutes that deploy at supersonic speeds

27
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:59,260
and low altitudes.

28
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:06,240
Just a wide range of conditions under which the parachutes

29
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:07,140
have to work.

30
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:13,820
These advances in parachute design and safety

31
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,920
gave birth to the intensely popular sport of skydiving,

32
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:22,760
with over 300,000 people jumping each year.

33
00:02:22,761 --> 00:02:25,660
To anyone who has wanted to break the bonds of Earth,

34
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:30,000
the enemy has always been gravity.

35
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:32,400
For if a person were to jump out of an airplane,

36
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:34,000
they would fall through the atmosphere

37
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:38,440
at a blistering rate of 110 miles per hour.

38
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:40,920
This speed, known as terminal velocity,

39
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:44,420
is the equilibrium speed of gravity and air resistance

40
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:47,120
exerted on the body.

41
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,780
But by using a parachute canopy to catch the air

42
00:02:50,781 --> 00:02:54,880
and increase the force called drag, gravity is countered,

43
00:02:54,980 --> 00:02:58,380
and the body is slowed to a leisurely speed of just over 1

44
00:02:58,480 --> 00:02:59,780
and 1 1 1 miles per hour.

45
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:08,000
Man has been trying to conquer gravity for centuries.

46
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:13,740
The earliest hint comes 900 years ago from ancient China.

47
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:17,340
As far as we know, some people were jumping off of walls

48
00:03:17,341 --> 00:03:22,640
and buildings, some structures with rigid canopies.

49
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:26,780
Probably looked like umbrellas or parasols.

50
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:30,320
Amidst the blossoming of the Italian Renaissance,

51
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:35,820
in 1495, Leonardo da Vinci made his famous sketch of a parachute.

52
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:38,600
As far as we know, he never made any working models.

53
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:40,000
But we do have a sketch.

54
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:42,100
And we know from that sketch, and from his other work,

55
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,140
that he certainly understood aerodynamics.

56
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:47,340
He understood that this parachute would work.

57
00:03:47,341 --> 00:03:52,340
In 1600, Italian Fausto Varanzio designed, built,

58
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,340
and demonstrated a parachute.

59
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:57,980
The one that Varanzio used in Venice

60
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:01,580
looked more like a large square sheet,

61
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,100
and it had rods in it.

62
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,300
All we have, of course, are woodcut sketches,

63
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:08,700
but we have reports that it was used.

64
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:15,200
Nearly 400 years later, base jumpers

65
00:04:15,201 --> 00:04:19,700
base jumpers take these early notions to extremes.

66
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:23,000
While they hope to escape the routine of everyday life,

67
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:25,340
Varanzio hoped to use his parachute

68
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:28,540
to escape from burning towers.

69
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:31,380
Ironically, it was the heat of hot air balloons

70
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:36,460
that really launched the world of parachute development.

71
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:42,220
The first actual manned flight occurred on November 21, 1783,

72
00:04:42,221 --> 00:04:45,620
when the Montgolfier brothers sent their balloon aloft.

73
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:48,200
With their balloonists floating above a startled crowd

74
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:50,560
of Parisians, the brothers opened the door

75
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:55,660
to an entire universe, the space above the Earth.

76
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:58,440
With the hot air balloon, daring adventurers who wanted

77
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:03,800
to jump by parachute had a much higher starting point.

78
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:07,440
One parachute pioneer, Jean-Pierre Blanchard,

79
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:10,640
experimented with dogs and farm animals.

80
00:05:10,641 --> 00:05:15,640
Accounts of the day report they landed safely.

81
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:18,040
The first serious human parachutist

82
00:05:18,140 --> 00:05:21,780
was another Frenchman, André-Jacques Garnarin.

83
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:24,480
He made a significant technological change away

84
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:29,200
from rigid structures to soft canvas.

85
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:35,300
On October 22, 1797, Garnarin by himself climbed into the basket

86
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,800
and began to ascend above Paris.

87
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:44,140
Garnarin was hanging beneath the balloon attached by a cord

88
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:45,980
to his parachute.

89
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:50,280
The canopy was made of 32 ribbed segments of white canvas

90
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:54,220
and stretched 23 feet in diameter when opened.

91
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:57,460
At 3,000 feet, he cut loose from the balloon

92
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:59,400
and parachuted back to Earth.

93
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:04,740
Garnarin was a sensation wherever he went,

94
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:07,760
making jump after jump.

95
00:06:07,761 --> 00:06:11,600
He always landed with a severe case of motion sickness

96
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:15,000
and often vomited after landing.

97
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:17,760
This was due to the fact that the air in the canopy

98
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:21,640
could only escape by swinging the parachute and Garnaren

99
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:24,140
side to side.

100
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,680
But Garnaren was undeterred.

101
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:29,740
He switched to using silk instead of canvas

102
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:32,980
because of its strength and durability.

103
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:36,720
He made more than 200 jumps in his lifetime.

104
00:06:36,721 --> 00:06:37,960
It was kind of barnstorming.

105
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:41,320
There was some money to be made there.

106
00:06:41,420 --> 00:06:43,120
Although I have to give them credit,

107
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:45,660
I think that they probably were doing it

108
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:50,660
for scientific purposes as well, just as any parachute tester

109
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:53,340
or test jumper does today.

110
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:57,540
One early experimenter died when his design failed.

111
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:00,540
In 1837, Englishman Robert Cocking

112
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:04,580
attempted a jump with an inverted cone canopy.

113
00:07:04,581 --> 00:07:09,760
The canopy collapsed, and cocking plummeted to his death.

114
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:12,580
German stunt jumping star Kathy Paulus

115
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:15,160
used the next dramatic change in parachutes

116
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:18,060
in the late 1800s.

117
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:20,500
Instead of hanging loosely, her canopy

118
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,300
was placed inside a pack hanging from the bottom of the balloon.

119
00:07:27,740 --> 00:07:31,080
When the Wright brothers achieved powered flight in 1903,

120
00:07:31,081 --> 00:07:34,380
they created a new, more controllable launch platform

121
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:36,860
for the parachute.

122
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:39,360
Early on, some people saw the necessity

123
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:42,060
of refining the parachute as a safety device

124
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:45,200
for the pilots of the airplanes.

125
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:49,240
On March 1, 1912, Captain Albert Berry,

126
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:51,900
a professional balloon parachute jumper,

127
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,340
became the first person to parachute from an airplane.

128
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,840
Berry's parachute was stuffed into a container

129
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:01,080
under the airplane.

130
00:08:01,081 --> 00:08:02,980
A fixed line called a static line

131
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:06,420
was attached from the plane to the parachute.

132
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:12,260
Barry's falling weight pulled the canopy out of its container.

133
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:14,920
But the first to manually open a parachute

134
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:20,400
and briefly experience free fall was 15-year-old Tiny Broadwick.

135
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:25,480
At a 1913 San Diego air show, Tiny's static line momentarily

136
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:29,480
got caught on one of the airplane's wheels.

137
00:08:29,481 --> 00:08:32,980
Before her next jump, she decided to cut the line.

138
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:37,580
When she jumped, she pulled the parachute open herself.

139
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:41,300
She landed safely on the ground and in the record books.

140
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:46,900
World War I saw the further intertwining

141
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,100
of parachutes and balloons.

142
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,040
The balloons changed in World War I.

143
00:08:51,140 --> 00:08:52,400
These were observation balloons.

144
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:54,040
They had to go up and stay up.

145
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:56,600
They were filled full of explosive hydrogen.

146
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:58,500
And many of them would be wearing the parachute

147
00:08:58,501 --> 00:08:59,760
and it was static line operated.

148
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:04,600
So the static line was hooked to the balloon.

149
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:07,340
If a plane came over the horizon

150
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:09,040
and was going to attack a balloon,

151
00:09:09,140 --> 00:09:11,440
there was no time to winch the balloon back down.

152
00:09:11,540 --> 00:09:14,820
And the observer in the basket had no choice but to simply

153
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:17,060
go over the side and get away from that thing

154
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:18,980
before it was hit by fire, because it would just

155
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:21,120
explode in the plane.

156
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:24,000
Surprisingly, the nemesis of balloon troops,

157
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:28,500
the World War I flying ace, did not wear a parachute.

158
00:09:28,501 --> 00:09:31,940
In England, military leaders were afraid the pilots would too

159
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:35,180
easily ditch their expensive airplanes.

160
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:37,880
Only at the end of the war did the German Air Force

161
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:41,620
equip its aces with life-saving parachutes,

162
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:44,320
like the static line-operated balloon parachutes.

163
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:51,420
Ace Ernst Udet, second only to the Red Baron in kills,

164
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:54,320
saved himself twice by floating back to Earth

165
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:57,220
after being shot down.

166
00:09:57,221 --> 00:09:59,120
After the war, the United States Army

167
00:09:59,220 --> 00:10:02,800
set up a permanent parachute program at McCook Field

168
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:04,620
in Dayton, Ohio.

169
00:10:04,720 --> 00:10:07,700
Major Edward Hoffman was the military chief,

170
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:10,040
Floyd Smith the civilian chief.

171
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:12,800
The result was the new Model A parachute.

172
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:18,200
The Model A was 28 feet in diameter

173
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:21,980
and had 40 suspension lines that connected the silk canopy

174
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:23,880
to the harness.

175
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:25,720
Instead of being stuffed in a container

176
00:10:25,721 --> 00:10:29,420
underneath the plane, the Model A was worn on the back.

177
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:32,120
This made leaving an airplane in trouble easier

178
00:10:32,220 --> 00:10:35,120
and increased the chances of saving the pilot's life.

179
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:41,240
Leslie Irvin, a parachute designer and stunt jumper,

180
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:43,340
was selected for the first live jump

181
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:45,700
of the new Model A parachute.

182
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:50,580
On April 28, 1919, Irvin jumped from a USD-9 biplane

183
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:55,720
flying 80 miles an hour at 1,500 feet over McCook field.

184
00:10:55,721 --> 00:10:59,600
He broke his leg on that jump, and he was so excited

185
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,520
that he went to the phone and called up a friend

186
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:07,740
in Buffalo, New York, and they started a parachute factory.

187
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:11,040
Ervin was given the nickname Sky High.

188
00:11:11,140 --> 00:11:15,300
For over 40 years, the basic design created by Floyd Smith

189
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:18,440
and his team at McCook Field dominated parachute

190
00:11:18,540 --> 00:11:20,580
manufacturing.

191
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:23,480
But doctors and army officials were still concerned

192
00:11:23,481 --> 00:11:26,680
concerned about a person falling through the air.

193
00:11:26,780 --> 00:11:29,580
They questioned whether someone would remain conscious and alert

194
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:31,420
through the ordeal.

195
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:33,060
Because of this, pilots were trained

196
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:35,460
to pull their ripcords and open their parachutes

197
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:39,960
immediately after leaving their stricken aircraft.

198
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:44,500
But on October 20, 1922, Lieutenant Harold R. Harris

199
00:11:44,600 --> 00:11:46,700
put these fears to rest.

200
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:49,680
Harris was testing a new Loaning Pursuit monoplane

201
00:11:49,681 --> 00:11:53,220
when it broke apart during flight at 2,500 feet

202
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:54,980
above the ground.

203
00:11:55,080 --> 00:11:57,080
Harris, wearing his Model A parachute,

204
00:11:57,180 --> 00:11:59,660
had trouble locating the ripcord.

205
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:04,160
He fell 2,000 feet, the first lengthy free fall in history

206
00:12:04,260 --> 00:12:06,900
before opening his parachute.

207
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Harris landed safely in a backyard grape arbor.

208
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:13,540
His chief complaint was the tear in his best pair of pants.

209
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:19,040
Lieutenant Harris became part of a growing club.

210
00:12:19,041 --> 00:12:22,480
people whose lives were saved by a parachute.

211
00:12:22,580 --> 00:12:24,780
The club became known as the Caterpillar Club,

212
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:27,820
named after the lowly creature responsible for making

213
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:30,620
the silken threads that saved lives.

214
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:34,280
Members received a certificate and a caterpillar pin.

215
00:12:34,380 --> 00:12:37,300
One famous member was Charles Lindbergh.

216
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:41,300
Before his historic solo flight across the Atlantic Ocean,

217
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:44,040
Lindbergh used parachutes four different times

218
00:12:44,140 --> 00:12:46,360
to escape from stricken airplanes.

219
00:12:46,361 --> 00:12:49,700
The parachute was just beginning to prove its worth.

220
00:12:54,300 --> 00:12:57,480
It takes 17,000 caterpillars eight weeks

221
00:12:57,580 --> 00:13:00,140
to make enough silk for one parachute.

222
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:02,680
Parachutes will return on Modern Marvels.

223
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:10,020
We now return to parachutes on Modern Marvels.

224
00:13:13,320 --> 00:13:15,920
As early as the final days of World War I

225
00:13:15,921 --> 00:13:18,560
military leaders considered the use of parachutes

226
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:20,320
for troop delivery.

227
00:13:20,420 --> 00:13:23,620
US General William Mitchell, in charge of Army Air Service

228
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:27,060
Forces in France, wanted to drop an entire division

229
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:28,860
behind enemy lines.

230
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:32,740
But the war was over before the plan could be put into action.

231
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:36,700
In 1930s Germany, a young aeronautical engineer

232
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:40,380
named Theo Kanaki reported to work in Stuttgart.

233
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:44,140
His boss assigned him to the new parachute group.

234
00:13:44,141 --> 00:13:46,060
He must have seen my disappointment.

235
00:13:46,380 --> 00:13:49,220
I had become an engineer to build the best airplane in the world

236
00:13:49,221 --> 00:13:50,980
and not to join the garment industry.

237
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:54,760
So I dropped my face by that Mr. Knarky said,

238
00:13:55,060 --> 00:13:56,240
let me tell you something here.

239
00:13:56,820 --> 00:14:01,040
I believe that using an engineering approach to parachutes

240
00:14:01,041 --> 00:14:03,700
and not only the jumper approach to parachute

241
00:14:03,701 --> 00:14:07,360
will make the parachute just as well a good piece of equipment

242
00:14:07,361 --> 00:14:11,520
as, for example, brakes on aircraft or flaps on aircraft

243
00:14:11,521 --> 00:14:12,580
or wings on aircraft.

244
00:14:12,581 --> 00:14:15,920
Kenocky lived up to his boss's expectations.

245
00:14:16,020 --> 00:14:19,220
An important part of the Nazi's successful blitzkrieg

246
00:14:19,320 --> 00:14:21,220
tactic was the use of paratroopers.

247
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:25,520
8,000 to 12,000 German soldiers jumped into Norway, Holland,

248
00:14:25,620 --> 00:14:28,960
and Belgium, proving that inserting soldiers by parachute

249
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:31,700
was both feasible and desirable.

250
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:34,740
The Germans pretty much invented the shock troops,

251
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:37,880
the blitzkrieg, getting in and out fast,

252
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:40,680
doing things in a real hurry, and rolling over the enemy.

253
00:14:40,681 --> 00:14:45,820
over the enemy, they saw the value in the parachute troops.

254
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:50,320
Then in May 1941 came Crete.

255
00:14:50,420 --> 00:14:54,100
The Nazis wanted to take control of the strategically located

256
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:56,800
Mediterranean island to prevent the British from using

257
00:14:56,900 --> 00:15:00,600
the island's three airports as a staging ground.

258
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:03,100
Lacking significant ocean-going troop ships,

259
00:15:03,200 --> 00:15:06,240
the Nazis relied heavily on landing soldiers from the air

260
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:09,240
by parachute and glider.

261
00:15:09,241 --> 00:15:13,920
Landing the gliders on little or no runway presented a difficult problem.

262
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:19,560
So German engineers proposed a two-parachute system to bring the gliders in.

263
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:24,000
We want to have a chute so that we can make a very steep approach,

264
00:15:24,100 --> 00:15:26,020
so we can hit the landing point much better.

265
00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:29,920
And then we want to have a second parachute that slows us down on landing.

266
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:33,580
And then we got the idea, now wait a minute, why don't we combine that?

267
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:38,760
We open the chute-only part on the approach and fully open in the landing,

268
00:15:38,761 --> 00:15:41,200
and that was the beginning of reefing parachuting.

269
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:48,240
A reefed canopy has a band wrapped around the lines,

270
00:15:48,340 --> 00:15:52,080
so that upon initial inflation, the canopy area is small,

271
00:15:52,180 --> 00:15:54,140
exerting less drag.

272
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:57,040
Controlled by the pilot or an automatic system,

273
00:15:57,140 --> 00:16:02,520
the disreefing allows the chute to inflate to its full diameter.

274
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:05,660
It was this reefing concept that gave Germany an advantage.

275
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:12,460
the Nazis committed 25,000 troops to capture Crete.

276
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:15,860
13,000 of them were landed by parachute or gliders

277
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:18,600
using the new reefed parachutes.

278
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:20,700
By conquering a force twice their number,

279
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:23,640
the Nazis proved the value of using massive numbers

280
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:25,940
of paratroopers.

281
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:29,480
With aviation technology advancing rapidly,

282
00:16:29,580 --> 00:16:32,860
Kanaki's group was handed another problem.

283
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:35,180
How to get a pilot safely out of a plane

284
00:16:35,181 --> 00:16:38,720
traveling just below supersonic speeds.

285
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:42,220
They came up with the first ejection seat.

286
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:45,320
We developed a seat that can be shot out of an aircraft

287
00:16:45,420 --> 00:16:48,120
because we will have very fast flying airplanes.

288
00:16:48,220 --> 00:16:51,120
I didn't know anything about jets at that time here.

289
00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:54,040
And we cannot bail out anymore like we do right now.

290
00:16:54,140 --> 00:16:56,440
We have to shoot them out.

291
00:16:56,540 --> 00:16:58,940
While the Germans were developing new technology

292
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:02,880
to deploy parachutes, Americans were creating new fabrics

293
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:05,180
to make parachutes.

294
00:17:05,181 --> 00:17:09,380
In the Pacific, the Japanese surprise attack on Pearl Harbor

295
00:17:09,381 --> 00:17:12,560
meant no more silk for American parachute manufacturers.

296
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:20,260
Fortunately, the 1938 DuPont invention of nylon,

297
00:17:20,261 --> 00:17:23,600
a petroleum-based synthetic proved successful

298
00:17:23,601 --> 00:17:25,740
as a substitute for silk.

299
00:17:25,741 --> 00:17:30,100
The army requisitioned the entire supply of nylon, 30 million tons

300
00:17:30,101 --> 00:17:33,040
to make parachutes.

301
00:17:33,041 --> 00:17:36,240
Packing the parachutes is of prime importance.

302
00:17:36,340 --> 00:17:38,540
The lines connecting the canopy to the harness

303
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:41,840
need to be straight and folded neatly.

304
00:17:41,940 --> 00:17:46,380
They're secured by rubber bands and allow for a staged opening.

305
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:49,360
Parachute failure can occur if the lines become entangled.

306
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:55,300
The parachute of choice by the United States Airborne Forces

307
00:17:55,400 --> 00:18:00,300
was the T-4, a 28-foot diameter round parachute.

308
00:18:00,301 --> 00:18:02,700
The T-4 was a static line parachute.

309
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:05,280
The soldiers falling weight pulled the canopy out

310
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:08,840
of the backpack, deploying the white chute.

311
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:13,120
The soldier had little control over the opening or the landing.

312
00:18:13,220 --> 00:18:17,480
The T-4 went where the wind carried it.

313
00:18:17,580 --> 00:18:20,180
While new materials were being developed for parachute

314
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:24,320
canopies, the soldiers that would wear them were being trained.

315
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:28,620
On August 15, 1942, the 82nd Infantry Division

316
00:18:28,621 --> 00:18:31,560
became the 82nd Airborne Division,

317
00:18:31,660 --> 00:18:33,900
the Army's first group of paratroopers.

318
00:18:37,200 --> 00:18:40,400
Their rapid training included the use of towers.

319
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:43,100
The soldier was placed in the parachute's harness,

320
00:18:43,200 --> 00:18:46,640
lifted to the top of the 250-foot tower, and then dropped.

321
00:18:49,500 --> 00:18:51,980
Next came an actual airplane drop,

322
00:18:52,080 --> 00:18:53,720
and then the paratrooper was ready.

323
00:18:53,721 --> 00:18:54,620
was ready.

324
00:18:58,620 --> 00:19:02,120
Throughout the war, the number of paratroop divisions grew,

325
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:04,360
so that by D-Day, there were four divisions

326
00:19:04,460 --> 00:19:07,600
with 50,000 paratroopers.

327
00:19:07,700 --> 00:19:11,840
Again, paratroopers greatly influenced a decisive battle.

328
00:19:11,940 --> 00:19:15,080
The success of D-Day June 6, 1944,

329
00:19:15,180 --> 00:19:19,040
was in no small part due to the effectiveness of the 82nd

330
00:19:19,140 --> 00:19:23,720
and the 101st airborne.

331
00:19:23,721 --> 00:19:29,520
of Allied troops by sea, 1,086 airplanes dropped more than 10,000

332
00:19:29,620 --> 00:19:32,220
Allied paratroopers behind German lines.

333
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:39,900
The landing of Allied troops on Utah Beach,

334
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,800
where airborne troops were dropped by parachute and glider

335
00:19:42,900 --> 00:19:46,540
ahead of the amphibious landing, resulted in only 12 troops

336
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:47,340
killed.

337
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:56,920
The landing of troops at neighboring Omaha Beach

338
00:19:57,020 --> 00:19:58,920
without the benefit of paratroopers

339
00:19:59,020 --> 00:20:05,260
resulted in a startling 2,374 Allied casualties.

340
00:20:09,340 --> 00:20:12,140
Throughout the rest of World War II,

341
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:15,240
paratroopers continue to play a significant role.

342
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:21,320
In the Pacific theater of war, paratroopers

343
00:20:21,321 --> 00:20:24,720
paratroopers jumped onto the strategic Japanese stronghold

344
00:20:24,820 --> 00:20:28,960
on Corregidor Island near Manila, the Philippine capital.

345
00:20:29,060 --> 00:20:31,160
Catching the Japanese by surprise,

346
00:20:31,260 --> 00:20:33,760
the island was liberated after a bitter battle.

347
00:20:36,760 --> 00:20:39,580
As US troops continued liberating Asia,

348
00:20:39,680 --> 00:20:42,680
paratroopers played a major role in freeing prisoners

349
00:20:42,780 --> 00:20:46,080
held by the Japanese at the Los Banos prison camp

350
00:20:46,180 --> 00:20:47,880
in the Philippines.

351
00:20:47,881 --> 00:20:51,680
In just 20 minutes, 2,000 men, women, and children

352
00:20:51,780 --> 00:20:52,580
were liberated.

353
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:00,620
For conquered people in Europe and Asia,

354
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:04,640
by the end of the war, sight of the sky filled with parachutes

355
00:21:04,740 --> 00:21:06,740
meant freedom was about to land.

356
00:21:11,140 --> 00:21:14,980
Of the 3.2 million skydiving jumps a year in the United

357
00:21:14,981 --> 00:21:19,660
Here in the United States, an average of 29 result in fatalities.

358
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:22,660
Parachutes will return on Modern Marvels.

359
00:21:27,460 --> 00:21:30,660
Now return to parachutes on Modern Marvels.

360
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:37,740
America's space program began with the end of World War II.

361
00:21:37,840 --> 00:21:41,140
Werner von Braun, head of Germany's V2 rocket group,

362
00:21:41,240 --> 00:21:44,680
came to America with many of his fellow German scientists

363
00:21:44,681 --> 00:21:46,380
engineers.

364
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:49,960
They knew how to get a rocket up and look to another former

365
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:54,660
German engineer to bring them safely back down.

366
00:21:54,760 --> 00:21:57,620
Theo Kanaki, parachute engineer, joined the group

367
00:21:57,720 --> 00:21:59,900
that spearheaded the development of parachutes

368
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,840
to bring astronauts back to Earth.

369
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:07,540
These parachutes, able to slow enormous objects like the Apollo

370
00:22:07,640 --> 00:22:12,240
11 command capsule weighing close to 11,000 pounds,

371
00:22:12,340 --> 00:22:14,540
are not closed canopies.

372
00:22:14,541 --> 00:22:15,540
They're ribbons.

373
00:22:18,540 --> 00:22:21,540
When we came to the idea to make a parachute of ribbon,

374
00:22:21,541 --> 00:22:23,140
we needed ribbon.

375
00:22:23,141 --> 00:22:27,180
There was a head manufacturer who used ribbon around for the heads,

376
00:22:27,181 --> 00:22:28,180
headband.

377
00:22:28,181 --> 00:22:30,180
They were 50 millimeters wide.

378
00:22:30,181 --> 00:22:32,800
50 millimeters happened to be two inches.

379
00:22:32,801 --> 00:22:36,100
And we demonstrated that it was possible as a landing

380
00:22:36,101 --> 00:22:39,640
in about October 1938 when we make the first landing

381
00:22:39,641 --> 00:22:42,340
with a nine-meter ribbon parachute behind the youngest

382
00:22:42,341 --> 00:22:44,140
aircraft, the W34.

383
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:48,480
Today, even the landing chute for the space shuttle

384
00:22:48,580 --> 00:22:52,880
has still 2-inch, 50-millimeter wide ribbon.

385
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:55,660
By allowing some air to slip through the gaps

386
00:22:55,760 --> 00:22:57,520
between the ribbons, the parachute

387
00:22:57,620 --> 00:23:01,020
captures just enough air to perform its task,

388
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:03,300
and not so much that it rips the parachute

389
00:23:03,400 --> 00:23:05,020
or destabilizes the vehicle.

390
00:23:07,560 --> 00:23:11,040
Despite the proven success of the ribbon parachute concept,

391
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:13,540
in the early days, there was some doubt

392
00:23:13,541 --> 00:23:17,440
There's some doubt about using parachutes to return astronauts

393
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:20,120
to Earth from outer space.

394
00:23:20,220 --> 00:23:23,420
I was asked to come to a meeting at NASA Langley.

395
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:27,360
The question was, would parachutes be safe enough

396
00:23:27,460 --> 00:23:29,620
to recover the astronauts?

397
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:31,700
Everybody had heard that parachutes once in a while

398
00:23:31,800 --> 00:23:33,520
don't open.

399
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:36,960
So the question was asked here, are parachutes really safe

400
00:23:37,060 --> 00:23:38,140
enough to do this?

401
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:39,340
I brought that data.

402
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:42,340
They never had a parachute that didn't open.

403
00:23:42,341 --> 00:23:45,580
this decade is out of landing a man on the moon

404
00:23:45,680 --> 00:23:47,680
and returning him safely to the Earth.

405
00:23:53,180 --> 00:23:55,820
The Apollo program was the most magnificent program

406
00:23:55,920 --> 00:23:57,520
I ever worked on in my life.

407
00:23:57,620 --> 00:23:59,120
It was a national goal.

408
00:23:59,220 --> 00:24:03,220
Kennedy had said we will land on the moon before 1970,

409
00:24:03,320 --> 00:24:04,860
and we did.

410
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:08,440
We worked here day and night, and we got it ready in time.

411
00:24:08,540 --> 00:24:10,440
We had in the Apollo space program

412
00:24:10,441 --> 00:24:15,140
So how did two consecutive successful manned landings?

413
00:24:15,240 --> 00:24:17,980
Before Mercury, Gemini, and Apollo,

414
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:21,960
when NASA was brand new, another program sent a man

415
00:24:22,060 --> 00:24:23,620
to the edge of space.

416
00:24:23,720 --> 00:24:27,520
In the late 50s, with new high-altitude supersonic jets

417
00:24:27,620 --> 00:24:30,160
like the U-2, the Air Force Command

418
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:32,420
needed to know if their pilots and personnel

419
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:35,900
could survive a long-distance fall.

420
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,340
They chose Air Force Captain Joe Kittinger, a test pilot,

421
00:24:39,341 --> 00:24:40,940
to help them find out.

422
00:24:41,040 --> 00:24:43,820
He was already fond of parachutes.

423
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:48,780
I was flying a F-100 in 1957.

424
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:52,420
And on takeoff, the engine caught on fire

425
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:54,760
and the airplane got out of control.

426
00:24:54,860 --> 00:24:59,200
And I ejected from 800 foot from an F-100.

427
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:01,500
So the parachute opened very low to the ground,

428
00:25:01,600 --> 00:25:03,160
but it saved my life.

429
00:25:03,260 --> 00:25:06,660
If it hadn't worked, I wouldn't be here talking to you.

430
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:08,440
Kittinger was placed in command

431
00:25:08,441 --> 00:25:11,180
of the Air Force's Project Man High

432
00:25:11,280 --> 00:25:13,840
to test the survivability of man and parachute

433
00:25:13,940 --> 00:25:15,680
at high altitudes.

434
00:25:15,780 --> 00:25:17,820
To reach the high altitudes, the Air Force

435
00:25:17,920 --> 00:25:20,520
went back to the early days of parachuting.

436
00:25:20,620 --> 00:25:23,060
They used a balloon.

437
00:25:23,160 --> 00:25:28,020
I made three high altitude parachute jumps, one at 76,000,

438
00:25:28,120 --> 00:25:31,700
one at 75,000, and one at 102,800 feet.

439
00:25:31,800 --> 00:25:36,100
So really three high altitude jumps.

440
00:25:36,101 --> 00:25:40,440
It was August 16, 1960, when Captain Kittinger took off

441
00:25:40,540 --> 00:25:43,800
in a balloon for an hour and a half ride, literally,

442
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:46,240
to the edge of outer space.

443
00:25:46,340 --> 00:25:49,240
Kittinger is straightforward about the reason for attempting

444
00:25:49,340 --> 00:25:52,020
what some people thought a suicide mission.

445
00:25:52,120 --> 00:25:53,020
There are accidents.

446
00:25:53,120 --> 00:25:53,980
There are tragedies.

447
00:25:54,080 --> 00:25:55,320
There are disasters that happen.

448
00:25:55,420 --> 00:25:57,360
And you have to develop systems to take care

449
00:25:57,460 --> 00:25:59,560
of the worst case environment.

450
00:25:59,660 --> 00:26:02,120
Any type of advancement that's made

451
00:26:02,220 --> 00:26:04,560
is made by little small steps.

452
00:26:04,561 --> 00:26:07,360
And our projects were just small steps

453
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:10,540
toward the accumulation of the knowledge that was ultimately

454
00:26:10,640 --> 00:26:14,300
used and is being used today.

455
00:26:14,400 --> 00:26:17,740
The temperature fell to 94 degrees below zero.

456
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:20,680
Kittinger was alone, the highest man on Earth

457
00:26:20,780 --> 00:26:24,320
in a tiny four-foot open gondola 19 and 1

458
00:26:24,420 --> 00:26:26,280
miles above the Earth.

459
00:26:26,380 --> 00:26:29,880
But it was his choice and his design.

460
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:32,420
They were going to have a pressurized gondola

461
00:26:32,421 --> 00:26:34,560
and have two people.

462
00:26:34,660 --> 00:26:36,960
And being a fighter pilot, I said, we don't need two people.

463
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:39,160
We just need one person.

464
00:26:39,260 --> 00:26:42,160
In his pressurized suit, he jumped out.

465
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:44,500
With the atmosphere painfully thin,

466
00:26:44,600 --> 00:26:48,640
Kittinger's blood would boil were his pressure suit to fail.

467
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:53,040
With the risk of thin atmosphere came the reward of speed.

468
00:26:53,140 --> 00:26:58,320
He fell from 103,000 feet to 90,000 feet in 16 seconds,

469
00:26:58,321 --> 00:27:02,900
Reaching a supersonic speed of 716 miles an hour,

470
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:06,260
the only human to go faster than the speed of sound

471
00:27:06,360 --> 00:27:08,160
without a vehicle.

472
00:27:08,260 --> 00:27:11,740
During his record free fall of 4 minutes and 36 seconds,

473
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:15,500
he was busier gathering information than impressions.

474
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:18,900
I was a test pilot, and I was there giving information

475
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,380
and data that we needed for the project.

476
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:23,680
So I was extremely busy.

477
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:26,340
And I watched constantly my altimeter, my stopwatch,

478
00:27:26,341 --> 00:27:29,280
my pressure suit pressures, the stabilization,

479
00:27:29,380 --> 00:27:30,440
how it was working.

480
00:27:30,540 --> 00:27:32,440
So I was a very, very busy person.

481
00:27:32,540 --> 00:27:33,980
No, I didn't write any poems.

482
00:27:34,080 --> 00:27:36,080
No, I didn't have any poetic thoughts.

483
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:39,420
I was a test pilot, and I was a parachutist there

484
00:27:39,520 --> 00:27:42,820
to gather the information that we needed.

485
00:27:42,920 --> 00:27:45,420
Kittinger's main parachute opened automatically

486
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:48,060
at 17,500 feet.

487
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:51,360
After a record eight minutes of descent under canopy,

488
00:27:51,460 --> 00:27:53,540
he was back on Earth.

489
00:27:53,541 --> 00:27:57,680
When I landed, I was so happy because we had demonstrated

490
00:27:57,780 --> 00:27:58,640
that we could do it.

491
00:27:58,740 --> 00:28:01,880
And we had worked for a year and a half, the same guys and I,

492
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:04,220
to demonstrate what we were doing.

493
00:28:04,320 --> 00:28:05,460
And here we had done it.

494
00:28:05,560 --> 00:28:07,620
And there were an awful lot of these people sitting around

495
00:28:07,720 --> 00:28:10,060
back in the bushes that said, well, you can never do this,

496
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:12,000
and so forth and so on.

497
00:28:12,100 --> 00:28:15,160
So we showed a lot of these people they were absolutely

498
00:28:15,260 --> 00:28:18,660
wrong because we did it in spite of what their projections

499
00:28:18,760 --> 00:28:19,860
were.

500
00:28:19,960 --> 00:28:22,340
Again, the miracle of the canopy

501
00:28:22,341 --> 00:28:26,580
demonstrated a new way to extend man's reach safely

502
00:28:26,680 --> 00:28:28,920
to the very edge of outer space.

503
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:38,160
Parachute literally means to shield against a fall.

504
00:28:38,260 --> 00:28:39,700
It derives from the French words

505
00:28:39,800 --> 00:28:41,960
parée, to shield, and shoot fall.

506
00:28:42,060 --> 00:28:43,460
Modern Marvels will return.

507
00:28:47,940 --> 00:28:51,140
We now return to Parachutes on Modern Marvels.

508
00:28:52,340 --> 00:28:57,340
In 1940, World War II was being fought in Europe.

509
00:28:57,440 --> 00:29:00,340
In order to learn how to train the enormous number

510
00:29:00,440 --> 00:29:03,880
of paratroopers the US Army anticipated needing,

511
00:29:03,980 --> 00:29:06,960
they turned to a fighter of another kind for help,

512
00:29:07,060 --> 00:29:10,820
the smoke jumpers, airborne firefighters who worked

513
00:29:10,920 --> 00:29:13,820
for the US Forest Service.

514
00:29:13,920 --> 00:29:18,840
In 1934, the Forest Service made the first test use

515
00:29:18,940 --> 00:29:20,240
of parachutes.

516
00:29:20,241 --> 00:29:22,440
made the first test use of parachutes

517
00:29:22,540 --> 00:29:25,080
to deliver firefighters by air.

518
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:27,380
The program grew out of necessity.

519
00:29:27,480 --> 00:29:29,080
The test was successful.

520
00:29:29,180 --> 00:29:31,080
And five years later, smoke jumping

521
00:29:31,180 --> 00:29:33,780
became full-time reality.

522
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:36,320
Originally in 39, when they started jumping, of course,

523
00:29:36,420 --> 00:29:37,400
it was essential.

524
00:29:37,500 --> 00:29:39,400
It took two days to hike people into a fire.

525
00:29:39,500 --> 00:29:41,360
And in that time, the fire could get really big.

526
00:29:41,460 --> 00:29:46,700
So a couple of guys could get there in half an hour

527
00:29:46,800 --> 00:29:48,140
and keep it that small.

528
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:52,640
Even now, with helicopters and a lot of roads,

529
00:29:52,740 --> 00:29:54,980
we can still respond very fast.

530
00:29:55,080 --> 00:29:58,280
That's our specialty, quick response for initial attack.

531
00:30:01,380 --> 00:30:04,280
In the early days, standard equipment for jumpers

532
00:30:04,380 --> 00:30:08,320
included a 30-foot round backpack parachute,

533
00:30:08,420 --> 00:30:12,760
a 27-foot chest emergency backup chute,

534
00:30:12,860 --> 00:30:17,000
a football helmet with wire mesh to protect against tree limbs,

535
00:30:17,001 --> 00:30:19,600
and various braces, ropes, shovels,

536
00:30:19,700 --> 00:30:21,880
and other firefighting tools.

537
00:30:21,980 --> 00:30:26,040
The salary for a smoke jumper was $193 a month,

538
00:30:26,140 --> 00:30:28,780
and the length of employment was 2 and 1 1 months.

539
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:33,480
Today, the Bureau of Land Management

540
00:30:33,580 --> 00:30:36,580
pays their smoke jumpers over $11 an hour

541
00:30:36,680 --> 00:30:38,820
for a season that lasts six months.

542
00:30:41,820 --> 00:30:44,520
Their training regimen includes classroom work

543
00:30:44,620 --> 00:30:45,820
and parachute practice.

544
00:30:45,821 --> 00:30:47,720
practice.

545
00:30:47,721 --> 00:30:50,420
To simulate real live jumps, the landing field

546
00:30:50,421 --> 00:30:52,320
is surrounded by tall trees.

547
00:30:54,560 --> 00:30:56,700
They're making sure their parachutes are working properly.

548
00:30:56,701 --> 00:30:57,960
They're going to do a right, left turn.

549
00:30:57,961 --> 00:31:01,200
They're going to do a stall check.

550
00:31:01,201 --> 00:31:03,280
I'd like to jump here because this is a really test

551
00:31:03,281 --> 00:31:05,680
of skills of the jumpers.

552
00:31:05,681 --> 00:31:08,280
Once they establish that their parachutes are OK to fly,

553
00:31:08,281 --> 00:31:10,240
they're going to start planning how they're

554
00:31:10,241 --> 00:31:12,920
going to make it in the spot.

555
00:31:12,921 --> 00:31:15,320
They need to be at a certain elevation

556
00:31:15,321 --> 00:31:16,580
and be over the spot.

557
00:31:16,680 --> 00:31:19,060
They don't want to be too low and be too far away from the spot.

558
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:24,560
The biggest challenge was the round parachute.

559
00:31:24,660 --> 00:31:27,700
While reliable at capturing air and slowing the smoke jumper

560
00:31:27,800 --> 00:31:31,000
down for a safe landing, it had little forward speed

561
00:31:31,100 --> 00:31:34,280
and was difficult to steer.

562
00:31:34,380 --> 00:31:36,880
The parachute used today by the smoke jumpers

563
00:31:36,980 --> 00:31:41,340
is a ram airtight parachute, sometimes called a parafoil.

564
00:31:41,440 --> 00:31:44,420
More of a wing than a canopy, the modern parachute

565
00:31:44,421 --> 00:31:47,220
allows for a significant degree of control.

566
00:31:47,320 --> 00:31:51,000
First patented in 1964 by Domino Jaubert,

567
00:31:51,100 --> 00:31:54,160
the Ram Air became the smokejumpers parachute of choice

568
00:31:54,260 --> 00:31:57,660
in 1982.

569
00:31:57,760 --> 00:32:01,000
Unlike the round canopy, which captures air,

570
00:32:01,100 --> 00:32:04,480
the rectangular canopy lets air pass above and below

571
00:32:04,580 --> 00:32:06,580
the surfaces.

572
00:32:06,680 --> 00:32:09,480
This design makes the canopy function exactly

573
00:32:09,580 --> 00:32:11,780
like an airplane wing.

574
00:32:11,880 --> 00:32:13,980
The difference between the rate of air passing

575
00:32:13,981 --> 00:32:16,820
over the top surface and under the bottom surface

576
00:32:16,920 --> 00:32:20,420
gives it lift and forward speed.

577
00:32:20,520 --> 00:32:25,160
A round canopy moves forward at a slow 5 to 7 miles an hour.

578
00:32:25,260 --> 00:32:28,660
The ram air flies at 20 miles an hour.

579
00:32:28,760 --> 00:32:30,960
Now, smoke jumpers can use that speed

580
00:32:31,060 --> 00:32:34,460
to steer themselves to a safe landing.

581
00:32:34,560 --> 00:32:36,900
By pulling down on the steering lines,

582
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:39,780
the aerodynamics of the canopy change,

583
00:32:39,781 --> 00:32:44,480
And the airflow guides the parachutist into a turn.

584
00:32:44,580 --> 00:32:46,820
Smoke jumpers vary from the college student

585
00:32:46,920 --> 00:32:48,520
to the returning veteran.

586
00:32:48,620 --> 00:32:50,220
They're committed firefighters who

587
00:32:50,320 --> 00:32:54,220
enjoy the added thrill of skydiving to work.

588
00:32:54,320 --> 00:32:57,340
Phil Brolier is one of the few smoke jumpers who returned

589
00:32:57,440 --> 00:32:59,440
to the job after decades away.

590
00:32:59,540 --> 00:33:01,440
PHIL BROLEIER, I only jumped three years just

591
00:33:01,540 --> 00:33:02,240
to get through college.

592
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:04,600
Had a family business that was an insurance agency that I took

593
00:33:04,700 --> 00:33:05,780
over from my dad.

594
00:33:05,880 --> 00:33:09,780
I was 40 years old, and I just said, I'm going back.

595
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:10,980
I appreciate the job more.

596
00:33:11,080 --> 00:33:12,820
Then it was a job to get me through college.

597
00:33:12,920 --> 00:33:14,520
Now it's just cool.

598
00:33:14,620 --> 00:33:17,420
It's just cool.

599
00:33:17,520 --> 00:33:20,560
He's passed the love of fighting fires and smoke jumping

600
00:33:20,660 --> 00:33:22,520
onto his son.

601
00:33:22,620 --> 00:33:25,000
I, uh, rickied in California in 95.

602
00:33:25,100 --> 00:33:27,500
I was there for two years and then transferred to Boise,

603
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:29,900
where my dad is, in 96.

604
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:33,300
When we were in the door jumping when we first did it,

605
00:33:33,400 --> 00:33:35,040
it's something you share with your kids,

606
00:33:35,140 --> 00:33:36,880
that you see their faces, their big eyes,

607
00:33:36,980 --> 00:33:39,040
they're looking at the jump spot, they're scared.

608
00:33:39,041 --> 00:33:41,940
and you're just looking at them and loving it.

609
00:33:42,040 --> 00:33:46,480
Unlike the old days, today not all smoke jumpers are men.

610
00:33:46,580 --> 00:33:47,780
It was really fun today.

611
00:33:47,880 --> 00:33:49,480
It was a really, there's no wind out here,

612
00:33:49,580 --> 00:33:52,380
so it was a pretty easy jump, actually.

613
00:33:52,480 --> 00:33:53,980
Our parachutes can do so much.

614
00:33:54,080 --> 00:33:56,620
My sister thinks I'm crazy, but my mom's

615
00:33:56,720 --> 00:33:58,720
finally getting used to the fact that I'm

616
00:33:58,820 --> 00:34:02,300
going to be doing this a while, so she's accepted it.

617
00:34:02,400 --> 00:34:04,200
Standard equipment for smoke jumpers

618
00:34:04,300 --> 00:34:07,140
include one main Ram Air parachute

619
00:34:07,141 --> 00:34:09,080
and one reserve parachute.

620
00:34:09,180 --> 00:34:11,040
In the event that the main chute fails,

621
00:34:11,140 --> 00:34:13,280
it needs to be cleared or released

622
00:34:13,380 --> 00:34:16,180
so that the reserve chute does not become entangled

623
00:34:16,280 --> 00:34:18,580
in the failed main chute.

624
00:34:18,680 --> 00:34:20,620
The system for ditching the main canopy

625
00:34:20,720 --> 00:34:22,760
is called the three ring system.

626
00:34:22,860 --> 00:34:26,120
It was invented by Bill Booth in 1979.

627
00:34:26,220 --> 00:34:30,500
This one single handle pulls this yellow cable out of a loop.

628
00:34:30,600 --> 00:34:33,060
There's two cables, one going to each side.

629
00:34:33,061 --> 00:34:37,160
So all I have to do is pull the cable free.

630
00:34:37,260 --> 00:34:39,100
And these rings flip through each other,

631
00:34:39,200 --> 00:34:40,960
and the main parachute is gone.

632
00:34:41,060 --> 00:34:44,200
These rings divide the force of an opening and your weight,

633
00:34:44,300 --> 00:34:45,900
so that the pull of this yellow cable

634
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:48,300
is just less than five pounds.

635
00:34:48,400 --> 00:34:51,140
Before Booth's system, the favorite method of releasing

636
00:34:51,240 --> 00:34:54,640
the main parachute was the Capewell system, designed

637
00:34:54,740 --> 00:34:58,160
originally for use after landing so a strong wind wouldn't drag

638
00:34:58,260 --> 00:35:00,460
a jumper across the ground.

639
00:35:00,461 --> 00:35:04,260
It was a slow two-step process involving both hands,

640
00:35:04,360 --> 00:35:06,140
and it sometimes malfunctioned.

641
00:35:06,240 --> 00:35:08,940
As a result, some parachutists died.

642
00:35:09,040 --> 00:35:10,740
Then came Booth.

643
00:35:10,840 --> 00:35:15,140
The three ring was a much better canopy release device.

644
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:21,720
So Bill Booth made a very, very important contribution there.

645
00:35:21,820 --> 00:35:24,220
Then, beginning in 1983, Booth

646
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:26,580
helped invent the tandem harness,

647
00:35:26,680 --> 00:35:29,560
allowing two people clipped together to skydive

648
00:35:29,561 --> 00:35:31,360
with one parachute.

649
00:35:31,460 --> 00:35:35,800
This helped open the floodgates of recreational skydiving.

650
00:35:35,900 --> 00:35:37,740
It's breathtaking up there.

651
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:40,200
Most people never get to leave the Earth.

652
00:35:40,300 --> 00:35:42,800
When you go up in an airplane, you're in an aluminum cylinder,

653
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:44,340
you stay in it and land.

654
00:35:44,440 --> 00:35:47,220
When you jump, it's like an astronaut doing a spacewalk.

655
00:35:47,320 --> 00:35:50,180
You get out there, and it's you in comparison

656
00:35:50,280 --> 00:35:52,780
to the rest of the world for the first time in your life.

657
00:35:59,560 --> 00:36:01,760
And when the free fall is over, the parachute opens,

658
00:36:01,860 --> 00:36:03,560
everything becomes quiet.

659
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:05,000
There's a feeling of floating.

660
00:36:05,100 --> 00:36:06,600
It's almost eerie.

661
00:36:06,700 --> 00:36:09,500
You can't figure out why you're just sitting there in the air,

662
00:36:09,600 --> 00:36:11,400
because up at 2,000 or 3,000 feet,

663
00:36:11,500 --> 00:36:14,940
there's no sensation of speed or falling.

664
00:36:15,040 --> 00:36:16,580
So I just find it beautiful.

665
00:36:21,520 --> 00:36:26,680
In more than 58 years, with over 130,000 jumps,

666
00:36:26,681 --> 00:36:30,380
Smoke jumpers have suffered only three fatalities.

667
00:36:30,480 --> 00:36:33,220
Parachutes will return on Modern Marvels.

668
00:36:36,620 --> 00:36:40,120
We now return to Parachutes on Modern Marvels.

669
00:36:44,540 --> 00:36:47,700
A new way of seeing things is the goal of the aerial

670
00:36:47,800 --> 00:36:51,000
or freefall cameraman.

671
00:36:51,100 --> 00:36:54,880
My job is to go out using the skills that I have

672
00:36:54,881 --> 00:36:59,880
as a skydiver and as a flyer to bring cameras

673
00:37:00,280 --> 00:37:02,880
to where the action is in the sky

674
00:37:02,881 --> 00:37:05,820
and to present it in the best way I can

675
00:37:05,821 --> 00:37:08,720
to let people on the ground experience it

676
00:37:08,721 --> 00:37:11,860
in a way that makes it feel like they were there.

677
00:37:11,861 --> 00:37:14,860
From a career as a sky surfing cameraman,

678
00:37:14,861 --> 00:37:17,160
Joe Jennings has broadened his view

679
00:37:17,161 --> 00:37:20,080
to include weightier subjects.

680
00:37:20,081 --> 00:37:23,340
I shot brand new Ford F-250s

681
00:37:23,341 --> 00:37:26,680
coming out of the back of a large military aircraft.

682
00:37:26,780 --> 00:37:28,780
We've all been in a car.

683
00:37:28,880 --> 00:37:30,880
But what's cool about the cars in free fall

684
00:37:30,980 --> 00:37:34,960
is the jumpers are actually flying in a car.

685
00:37:35,060 --> 00:37:36,660
And it's real.

686
00:37:36,760 --> 00:37:38,660
While some use parachutes to push

687
00:37:38,760 --> 00:37:41,660
the visual state of the art, there

688
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:44,400
are others pushing the life-saving state of the art.

689
00:37:47,160 --> 00:37:50,640
Joe Crotwell is a legend among test parachutists

690
00:37:50,641 --> 00:37:56,020
for his work on standard setting stress tests in the 1970s.

691
00:37:56,120 --> 00:37:58,840
Now he runs the Aerospace Equipment Research

692
00:37:58,940 --> 00:38:00,620
Organization.

693
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:02,460
Today we're doing a 600 knot test.

694
00:38:02,560 --> 00:38:05,780
We'll fly across the range at approximately 1,500 feet

695
00:38:05,880 --> 00:38:08,320
AGL above the ground.

696
00:38:08,420 --> 00:38:11,060
And then we'll fire the seat over the middle of the range.

697
00:38:11,160 --> 00:38:12,720
And then we'll see what happens.

698
00:38:12,820 --> 00:38:15,640
China Lake Naval Air Warfare Center

699
00:38:15,740 --> 00:38:17,900
is the site of a parachute test.

700
00:38:17,901 --> 00:38:21,500
An F-4 is the flying test bed for a new ejection seat

701
00:38:21,600 --> 00:38:25,840
parachute to be used in certain F-18 jet fighters.

702
00:38:25,940 --> 00:38:29,580
Joe Crotwell is spearheading the test.

703
00:38:29,680 --> 00:38:32,440
This is a 28-foot parachute compared

704
00:38:32,540 --> 00:38:33,980
to the parachute that came in this seat

705
00:38:34,080 --> 00:38:35,580
that has a 17-foot diameter.

706
00:38:35,680 --> 00:38:38,120
So this is a substantially bigger canopy with a much slower

707
00:38:38,220 --> 00:38:39,660
rate of descent.

708
00:38:39,760 --> 00:38:42,300
The larger parachute means a softer landing

709
00:38:42,400 --> 00:38:44,800
in the event an ejection is necessary.

710
00:38:44,900 --> 00:38:47,360
For today's test, the passenger in the rear seat

711
00:38:47,361 --> 00:38:48,560
is a test dummy.

712
00:39:02,240 --> 00:39:05,740
The recovery team inspects the parachute and the test dummy

713
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:09,580
where they land before transporting them back to the lab

714
00:39:09,680 --> 00:39:12,960
for further analysis.

715
00:39:13,060 --> 00:39:16,080
We thought that we could integrate these two products,

716
00:39:16,081 --> 00:39:17,340
And now we've proved it.

717
00:39:17,440 --> 00:39:20,220
And there's no way you can get a bad feeling from that.

718
00:39:20,320 --> 00:39:22,580
So when we had that successful ejection today,

719
00:39:22,680 --> 00:39:24,820
obviously, we're all walking 10 feet off the ground.

720
00:39:27,320 --> 00:39:29,820
While parachutes used in existing aircraft

721
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:34,100
are constantly being improved, new uses of parachutes

722
00:39:34,200 --> 00:39:36,940
for new situations are also being tested.

723
00:39:37,040 --> 00:39:44,080
Mark, we got five, four, three, two, one.

724
00:39:44,081 --> 00:39:46,480
Launch, launch, launch.

725
00:39:46,580 --> 00:39:51,620
Roy Fox is working on NASA's X-38, a crew return vehicle

726
00:39:51,720 --> 00:39:55,160
for the International Space Station.

727
00:39:55,260 --> 00:39:56,460
It's a rescue vehicle.

728
00:39:56,560 --> 00:39:58,260
Seven astronauts can climb in it.

729
00:39:58,360 --> 00:40:00,900
The lifting body is good for reentering the atmosphere,

730
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:04,440
but it's something that has to be landed at a very high speed,

731
00:40:04,540 --> 00:40:06,940
like 260 miles an hour at touchdown.

732
00:40:07,040 --> 00:40:09,380
We have put a big parafoil, a big ram

733
00:40:09,480 --> 00:40:11,140
air inflated parachute on top.

734
00:40:11,240 --> 00:40:13,240
We don't have calls.

735
00:40:13,241 --> 00:40:16,780
The parafoil that carries the X-38 safely back to Earth

736
00:40:16,880 --> 00:40:23,780
is 5,500 square feet, 45 feet by 120 feet.

737
00:40:23,880 --> 00:40:27,020
It lands under this big parafoil, which allows it

738
00:40:27,120 --> 00:40:29,060
to have a very soft touchdown.

739
00:40:29,160 --> 00:40:30,620
It doesn't need a pilot.

740
00:40:30,720 --> 00:40:32,920
The computer-controlled robotic landing

741
00:40:33,020 --> 00:40:36,800
vehicle is pushing the limits of parachute design,

742
00:40:36,900 --> 00:40:38,540
creating the state of the art.

743
00:40:38,541 --> 00:40:44,420
But there are perhaps no more challenging parachuting

744
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:48,280
conditions than the ones to be found on other planets

745
00:40:48,380 --> 00:40:50,060
and moons.

746
00:40:50,160 --> 00:40:53,580
While Fox and the X-38 are focused on Earth and Earth

747
00:40:53,680 --> 00:40:56,260
orbit, the Jet Propulsion Laboratory

748
00:40:56,360 --> 00:41:00,700
is using parachutes to extend man's knowledge of other planets.

749
00:41:00,800 --> 00:41:05,660
The Stardust mission is on its way to Comet Vilt 2

750
00:41:05,661 --> 00:41:08,100
to collect particles from the comet's tail

751
00:41:08,200 --> 00:41:10,100
and return them for study to Earth

752
00:41:10,200 --> 00:41:12,640
using a parachute-controlled descent.

753
00:41:12,740 --> 00:41:14,140
See ya.

754
00:41:14,240 --> 00:41:16,240
Tests of the return descent module

755
00:41:16,340 --> 00:41:19,280
are conducted by dropping a mock-up from a hot air balloon.

756
00:41:19,380 --> 00:41:20,680
Oh, there it comes.

757
00:41:20,780 --> 00:41:21,780
There it comes.

758
00:41:29,880 --> 00:41:32,420
In 1997, the Pathfinder mission

759
00:41:32,421 --> 00:41:35,320
used a single 24-foot diameter parachute

760
00:41:35,420 --> 00:41:37,360
to slow the spacecraft.

761
00:41:37,460 --> 00:41:40,060
It deployed two to three minutes after entry

762
00:41:40,160 --> 00:41:43,960
into the thin Martian atmosphere.

763
00:41:44,060 --> 00:41:47,760
Soon the roving sojourner was sending pictures back to Earth.

764
00:41:51,380 --> 00:41:54,780
Traveling at 106,000 miles an hour,

765
00:41:54,880 --> 00:41:59,780
the 747-pound Galileo probe achieved the highest impact

766
00:41:59,781 --> 00:42:04,020
impact speed of any man-made object when it hit the atmosphere

767
00:42:04,120 --> 00:42:06,820
of Jupiter.

768
00:42:06,920 --> 00:42:10,920
The probe then deployed a Dacron canopy to slow it down

769
00:42:11,020 --> 00:42:14,220
so the seven onboard instruments could gather information.

770
00:42:18,040 --> 00:42:21,840
The Cassini mission to Saturn includes the Huygens probe,

771
00:42:21,940 --> 00:42:26,240
destined for a soft landing via parachute onto the surface

772
00:42:26,241 --> 00:42:28,880
of our solar system's largest moon, Titan.

773
00:42:31,880 --> 00:42:34,640
We expect these parachutes to work and take

774
00:42:34,740 --> 00:42:36,780
their miracles for granted.

775
00:42:36,880 --> 00:42:39,620
But there are still many unknowns about the parachute

